2026-06-14 13:58:57.532 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Island mode is architecturally relevant to T0804 (Block Reporting Message) but offers limited reliable prevention of the technique itself. While islanding can theoretically halt lateral movement and external communication disruptions, it does not meaningfully address the core mechanism of T0804, which is an adversary blocking or preventing reporting messages from reaching their intended target within a compromised environment.\n\nSpecific limitations:\n\n1. **Does not prevent intra-system message blocking**: Island mode assumes the OT network is severed from external systems, but T0804 attacks typically occur within the operational environment itself—an adversary already present in the islanded network can continue to block local reporting messages (e.g., preventing HMI updates, blocking messages between PLCs and the local control center).\n\n2. **Post-compromise reactive measure**: Island mode is a response to detect a severe incident, but by the time islanding is triggered, an adversary may have already achieved the blocking objective. Additionally, if the adversary is internal to the islanded segment, islanding does nothing to stop ongoing message blocking attacks.\n\n3. **Does not restore message integrity or detection**: The control does not include mechanisms to validate reporting message authenticity, detect message tampering, or ensure messages reach operators—it only severs external connectivity. Operators within the islanded environment may still be denied critical telemetry.\n\n4. **Limited operational visibility restoration**: While islanding ensures local processes continue, it may actually worsen situation awareness if adversaries have already disabled reporting mechanisms, since external monitoring and remote support are disabled.\n\nIsland mode provides general defense-in-depth by containing the threat, but it does not functionally stop the blocking of reporting mes