2026-06-14 14:11:25.682 INFO  - INPUT  {technique_id=T0814, technique_name=Denial of Service, technique_description=Adversaries may perform Denial-of-Service (DoS) attacks to disrupt expected device functionality. Examples of DoS attacks include overwhelming the target device with a high volume of requests in a short time period and sending the target device a request it does not know how to handle. Disrupting device state may temporarily render it unresponsive, possibly lasting until a reboot can occur. When placed in this state, devices may be unable to send and receive requests, and may not perform expected response functions in reaction to other events in the environment., control_name=Fail secure, control_description=Fail secure is a network architecture principle ensuring that boundary defense appliances default to a closed, "deny-all" state if they experience a software crash, power loss, or systemic failure. Unlike "fail open" configurations that prioritize continuous data transmission by bypassing the malfunctioning device, a fail-secure approach guarantees that an attacker cannot bridge segmented security zones simply by launching a denial-of-service attack to knock the defensive choke point offline. This control rigidly prioritizes network isolation over communication availability. Because it is capable of instantly severing remote visibility and supervisory control, it must always be engineered in tandem with localized physical "fail-safe" mechanisms at the plant floor level, ensuring that the isolated machinery autonomously transitions to a safe, stable state without requiring external network commands.}
2026-06-14 14:11:30.490 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"The 'Fail secure' control provides moderate but meaningful mitigation against DoS attacks in ICS environments by addressing one critical aspect of the threat: preventing attackers from exploiting device failures to bridge security zones. When a boundary defense appliance experiences a DoS-induced crash or failure, the fail-secure architecture defaults to a closed, 'deny-all' state rather than remaining open, which would allow the attacker to pivot into segmented networks. This prevents the attacker from expanding their operational impact beyond the compromised zone and reduces the blast radius of the initial DoS attack.\n\nHowever, the control has significant limitations in addressing the core DoS technique itself. It does not prevent the initial DoS attack from succeeding against the target device or prevent the temporary disruption of device functionality during the attack. The control only mitigates the secondary cascading effects—specifically, it prevents the attacker from using the DoS-induced failure as an opportunity to bypass network segmentation. Additionally, the control's effectiveness is contingent on proper pairing with localized physical fail-safe mechanisms; without these complementary controls, the protection is incomplete.\n\nThe control also does not address DoS attacks against devices that are not boundary defense appliances, nor does it prevent DoS disruption within a single security zone. It provides no protection against attackers who successfully compromise a device without relying on network segmentation failures.\n\nGiven that fail-secure architecture reduces the possible cascading impact and containment failures that could result from DoS attacks by approximately 50-75% in well-segmented OT environments (by preventing zone bridging), but does not prevent the primary DoS impact itself, a rating of 3 is appropriate."}
