2026-06-14 14:11:40.013 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Fail secure provides limited mitigation against T0813 (Denial of Control) by addressing only a narrow subset of attack vectors. The control prevents attackers from exploiting boundary defense failures to gain unauthorized control access, which could theoretically prevent some denial-of-control scenarios initiated through network-based attacks that compromise perimeter devices. However, this protection is indirect and conditional.\n\nThe control's effectiveness is significantly constrained because T0813 encompasses diverse attack mechanisms: direct compromise of control systems, man-in-the-middle attacks on control communications, resource exhaustion attacks on control interfaces, and manipulation of control device firmware or software. Fail secure only mitigates scenarios where denial of control stems from an attacker exploiting a failed or compromised boundary defense appliance specifically. It does not address:\n\n1. Direct attacks on control systems within the network (which remain unprotected once past the boundary)\n2. Insider threats or compromised credentials used to directly interact with controls\n3. Application-layer attacks targeting the control interface itself\n4. Resource exhaustion or flooding attacks against control systems\n5. Manipulation of control devices through legitimate but compromised pathways\n\nWhile the control's requirement for localized physical fail-safe mechanisms is operationally sound, the fail-secure boundary architecture itself only reduces blast radius for a specific class of network-based denial attacks (those exploiting failed boundary appliances). In a typical OT environment with multiple attack pathways to control systems, this represents approximately 25-50% reduction in overall impact potential, as many denial-of-control attacks would still succeed through alternative vectors not dependent on boundary appliance compromise. The control is a