2026-06-14 14:11:52.001 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":1,"coverage_justification":"Fail secure is architecturally relevant to T0815 (Denial of View) but offers limited direct prevention. The control addresses one narrow vector where an attacker might exploit a failed boundary defense appliance to disrupt visibility, but this represents only a fraction of possible denial of view attacks. Fail secure's primary benefit here is preventing attackers from leveraging a compromised or failed firewall/gateway to maintain visibility disruption across network segments. However, the technique encompasses numerous other denial of view mechanisms that fail secure does not address: direct interference with communication channels, sensor/device compromise that prevents status message transmission, man-in-the-middle attacks that suppress messages, protocol manipulation, or targeting the supervisory control interface directly. Additionally, the control creates an inherent operational trade-off: by defaulting to a 'deny-all' state during failure, fail secure may itself cause denial of view to operators when the boundary appliance fails (even without adversary involvement), potentially degrading visibility rather than improving it. While the control mitigates one specific attack path (boundary device compromise leading to visibility disruption), it does not meaningfully reduce the overall blast radius or impact of denial of view techniques across the broader ICS environment. The reliance on localized fail-safe mechanisms is also only relevant if the attack targets remote supervisory control—it does not address denial of view attacks that affect local operator interfaces or status monitoring systems."}
