2026-06-14 14:12:26.095 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Fail secure provides limited but meaningful containment of T0831 (Manipulation of Control) through network-level isolation mechanisms. The control's primary value lies in its ability to sever remote supervisory command channels when boundary defense appliances fail, which prevents attackers from remotely manipulating control parameters through compromised network paths. However, the coverage is significantly constrained by several factors: (1) Fail secure primarily protects against remote manipulation by blocking network communication flows during failures, but does not prevent manipulation that occurs after an attacker has already gained local access to control systems or process networks; (2) The technique explicitly includes manipulation via direct communication with control devices ('communicate with and command physical control processes'), which may bypass network boundaries entirely; (3) Fail secure requires complementary 'fail-safe' mechanisms at the plant floor level to ensure safe autonomous operation—without these, isolated machinery could still be manipulated through local access or pre-planted malicious logic; (4) The control does not address manipulation of set point values, tags, or parameters that occur within already-compromised control networks or through compromised operator interfaces, which represent common attack vectors; (5) An attacker who achieves code execution on a control system before the fail-secure boundary activates can execute manipulation commands. The control reduces blast radius by preventing sustained remote command-and-control after network failure and by enforcing isolation zones, which limits an attacker's ability to propagate manipulation across network segments, but it does not prevent the initial manipulation act itself or manipulation within already-segmented zones. This justifies a rating of 2, as the control provides some impact reducti