2026-06-14 14:12:30.906 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":2,"coverage_justification":"Fail secure provides limited but meaningful mitigation against Loss of Control attacks. The control addresses the defensive boundary layer by ensuring that network segmentation cannot be bypassed through DoS attacks against perimeter devices—this prevents attackers from establishing a persistent control channel that would sustain loss of operator control. However, the control's effectiveness is significantly constrained by several factors: (1) Loss of Control can be achieved through direct manipulation of control system logic, PLC firmware, or field devices rather than network-level attacks, which fail secure does not address; (2) the control only protects the perimeter and assumes attackers have already achieved internal access, which is typical in targeted ICS attacks like the steel mill example cited; (3) the reliance on localized 'fail-safe' mechanisms at the plant floor is mentioned as a tandem requirement but is outside the scope of this control itself—fail secure alone cannot enforce safe state transitions without these complementary physical safeguards; (4) once an adversary has compromised internal control systems, fail secure cannot prevent sustained loss of control by blocking legitimate operator commands or inducing runaway conditions in process parameters. The control reduces impact by preventing one specific attack vector (sustained control loss via external network interference) but does not prevent the core technique of achieving sustained loss of operator command authority through internal compromise. This represents approximately 25-40% impact reduction against the full spectrum of Loss of Control attack scenarios."}
