2026-06-14 14:27:19.719 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":3,"coverage_justification":"Separation of Concerns provides moderate-to-good coverage against Unauthorized Command Message attacks by architecturally limiting the blast radius and preventing cascading compromise, but does not directly prevent the technique itself. Here's the detailed analysis:\n\nStrengths:\n1. Network Segmentation Impact: By strictly isolating BPCS from SIS and segmenting operational zones per Purdue/IEC 62443 models, this control prevents unauthorized command messages from traversing between critical domains. An attacker compromising one zone cannot easily send commands to isolated control systems in another zone.\n2. Reduces Attack Surface: Compartmentalization means fewer interconnected systems can receive or process commands from a potentially compromised source, limiting which assets can be targeted with unauthorized messages.\n3. Blast Radius Containment: Even if an attacker successfully injects an unauthorized command into one zone, the impact is confined to that zone. A compromised SCADA segment cannot send commands to safety-critical SIS functions, significantly limiting operational disruption.\n4. SIS Protection: The absolute isolation between BPCS and SIS ensures that even if primary control is compromised by unauthorized commands, safety shutdown mechanisms remain functional and independent.\n\nWeaknesses:\n1. Does Not Prevent Command Injection: This control does not prevent an attacker from crafting or injecting unauthorized commands within a compromised zone. It only prevents those commands from spreading across architectural boundaries.\n2. Intra-Zone Vulnerability: Within a single operational zone (e.g., within the BPCS itself), unauthorized command messages can still propagate between devices unless additional controls like authentication, command validation, or protocol-level filtering are implemented.\n3. Assumes Proper Implementation: The effectiveness depends on rigorous physical and logical isolation; misconfigured segmentation, poorly managed zone boundaries, or legacy system interconnections can undermine the control.\n4. Does Not Address Root Cause: The control mitigates consequences but does not directly detect, validate, or block malicious commands—it only constrains their reach.\n\nNet Impact: In a typical OT environment with best-in-class segmentation, this control reduces the operational impact by preventing cross-zone command propagation and protecting critical safety functions, resulting in approximately 50-75% reduction in potential blast radius and cascading failures. However, because unauthorized commands can still be successfully transmitted and executed within compromised zones, it does not fully prevent the technique."}
