2026-06-14 16:17:52.986 INFO  - OUTPUT {"coverage_rating":4,"coverage_justification":"Interlocks provide strong mitigation against Loss of Control (T0827) by preventing equipment from entering hazardous or runaway states through independent physical or electrical mechanisms that operate outside the ICS network. When properly implemented, interlocks can enforce safe operational prerequisites and prevent dangerous equipment states even when control system commands are compromised or malicious. This directly addresses the core threat of T0827—preventing operators from regaining control—by ensuring that certain unsafe conditions simply cannot occur regardless of attacker commands.\n\nThe control achieves a rating of 4 rather than 5 because: (1) Interlocks are typically point solutions protecting specific equipment interactions rather than all possible loss-of-control scenarios; (2) Their effectiveness depends on proper design, installation, and maintenance—poorly designed or maintained interlocks may fail or be bypassed; (3) Interlocks primarily prevent entry into unsafe states but do not actively help operators regain control once a loss of control event begins; (4) Sophisticated attackers may find ways to disable or circumvent interlocks if they have sufficient low-level access, or may target equipment not protected by interlocks; (5) Interlocks cannot mitigate loss of control in scenarios where the malicious state itself is technically 'safe' but operationally damaging (e.g., a controlled shutdown when production is needed).\n\nNonetheless, in a typical OT environment with best-in-class interlock coverage protecting critical equipment interactions, this control can reduce the operational impact of loss-of-control attacks by 75-90% by preventing the most severe and dangerous runaway conditions from occurring."}
